  PIPELINE
}

/// The lore bible's entry kinds. A writer opening a scene needs to know what is
/// already canon before inventing more, so a node's references point at these
/// rather than at free text.
enum StoryEntryKind {
  THEME
  REGION
  CREATURE
  CHARACTER
  FACTION
  ITEM
  EVENT
  RULE
  /// A consequence with a canonical name — set by one quest, checked by
  /// another, possibly chapters apart. Flags exist so two writers can never
  /// spell the same consequence two ways; the set and check ends both
  /// reference the entry, and the bible shows everywhere it appears.
  FLAG
  /// A game mechanic written down for the writers' room — what the player can
  /// actually do — so quests are written toward systems that exist. Its meta
  /// carries the release gate: the arc that switches the system on.
  SYSTEM
  /// An evolving narrative concept — an arc, a mystery, a betrayal, an ending
  /// — worked like a brainstorm. Its development status (brainstorming →
  /// approved → implemented) lives in meta, its discussion in comments, and
  /// the export never carries it: an implemented thread ships as real arcs
  /// and entries, not as the argument that produced them.
  THREAD
  /// One mission in a companion's personal chain — a first-class record with
  /// its own status, order, unlock conditions, and consequences, so a
  /// companion arc is nine linked dossiers rather than a wall of text on the
  /// character. Withheld from the export for the same reason threads are.
  COMPANION_MISSION
}

/// Contribution status. Anyone below ADMIN writes at PROPOSED, and only CANON
/// is ever exported to the game, so an unreviewed idea cannot reach an Unreal
/// build. REJECTED is kept rather than deleted: a rejected branch is a decision
/// worth remembering, and the author should be able to see what happened to it.
enum StoryStatus {
  DRAFT
  PROPOSED
  CANON
  REJECTED
  ARCHIVED
}

enum StoryNodeKind {
  BEAT
  SCENE
  DIALOGUE
  CHOICE
  CONDITION
  QUEST_START
  QUEST_STEP
  ENDING
}

/// The valence of an ENDING node, carried to the game so the importer can set
/// the quest state machine's success/failure node type. NEUTRAL is a real
/// answer, not a default — the prologue's Defend-or-Flee endings are neither
/// victory nor defeat by authorial intent.
enum StoryEndingKind {
  SUCCESS
  FAILURE
  NEUTRAL
}

/// What kind of story an arc is — the categories the room actually files by:
/// the mainline chapters, side quests, the contracts posted at a place, a
/// companion’s own quest, an incursion, a world event.
///
/// `StoryArc.isMainline` stays alongside this and stays in lockstep. The
/// export contract, the arc ordering, and every arc picker read the boolean,
/// and a CHECK constraint refuses any row where the two disagree — so a writer
/// that sets one column and forgets the other fails loudly instead of shipping
/// a mainline chapter the game reads as a side quest.
enum StoryArcCategory {
  MAINLINE
  SIDE_QUEST
  CONTRACT
  COMPANION_QUEST
  FACTION_QUEST
  INCURSION
  WORLD_EVENT
}

/// Why an assistant exchange ended the way it did. Every outcome is recorded,
/// including the ones where no request ever reached Gemini — an audit that only
/// kept successful answers would hide exactly the pattern worth looking for.
enum StoryAssistantOutcome {
  ANSWERED
  BLOCKED
  RATE_LIMITED
  BUDGET_EXHAUSTED
  UNAVAILABLE
  UNCONFIGURED
}

enum StoryRevisionAction {
  CREATED
  UPDATED
  MOVED
  STATUS_CHANGED
  DELETED
  LINKED
  UNLINKED
}

/// Geometry is deliberately small and engine-neutral. The authored atlas uses
